/SourceModified (D:20251029053604)
/abstract (High-resolution remote sensing change detection \(RSCD\) plays a crucial role in various applications such as urban expansion monitoring and disaster assessment. However, traditional methods often face difficulties with high-resolution data due to atmospheric variations, seasonal changes, and intra-class heterogeneity. While deep learning techniques, particularly convolutional neural networks \(CNNs\) and Transformer-based models, have shown promise, they still struggle with either computational inefficiency or limitations in capturing complex spatial features in large-scale, high-resolution imagery.In this paper, we propose a novel GPU-based method that leverages a Multi-Scale Context Aggregation \(MSCA\) module to address these challenges. Our approach integrates parallel convolutions with varying dilation rates, image-level pooling, and differential feature processing to expand the receptive field while retaining fine spatial details. This enables our method to effectively model both local changes and long-range contextual relationships without the heavy computational cost of Transformer-based models.Experimental results on the Google dataset demonstrate that our method outperforms existing state-of-the-art models, achieving the highest IoU \(88.32%\), F1-score \(93.75%\), and Precision \(93.45%\). Visual analyses further confirm the superiority of our method, showcasing its ability to detect changes with minimal false positives. Loss curve analysis also shows steady convergence, highlighting the stability and efficiency of our model. Our GPU-based MSCA module offers a significant advantage in handling high-resolution RSCD tasks, providing both high accuracy and computational efficiency.)
